<BaseLayout {title} {description} path="/guides/mouse-wheel-jumping" type="article"><JsonLd data={{'@context':'https://schema.org','@type':'Article',headline:title,description,url:absoluteUrl('/guides/mouse-wheel-jumping')}}/><Breadcrumbs items={[{label:'Guides',href:'/guides/how-to-test-mouse-wheel'},{label:'Mouse Wheel Jumping'}]}/><article class="content"><h1>{title}</h1><p class="lead">A jumping wheel can feel like a page skips, jitters, or briefly moves in the opposite direction. The pattern matters more than one isolated jump.</p><p>Use the <a href="/">Scroll Wheel Test</a> to see the browser-visible signal while you make a steady roll. Red portions of the graph are events opposite to your chosen direction.</p><h2>What can cause wheel jumping?</h2><p>The wheel’s encoder may be contaminated or worn, but intermittent wireless connections, battery level, driver utilities, and page-specific behavior can look similar. A high-resolution wheel or trackpad can also generate more varied events than a traditional notched wheel.</p><h2>How to narrow it down</h2><p>First test in a second browser. Next, reconnect the device or move its receiver closer. If the pattern remains, test another mouse on the same computer and your mouse on another computer. That comparison separates a device pattern from a system pattern better than guessing from one app.</p><h2>Do not over-read one test</h2><p>Browser tools cannot inspect the physical encoder. Repeat the <a href="/">scroll wheel test</a> two or three times before deciding whether there is a consistent reverse or jitter signal.</p><RelatedTools current="wheel"/></article></BaseLayout>
